   "run": {
    "status": "answered",
    "final": "The `systemctl --failed` command is used to list all systemd units that have failed. Here's how to use it:\n\n## Basic Usage\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\nThis will display a list of all units that have failed, showing their names and status.\n\n## Output Format\n\nThe output typically shows:\n- **UNIT** - The name of the failed unit\n- **LOAD** - Whether the unit configuration was loaded\n- **ACTIVE** - Whether the unit is active\n- **SUB** - The substate of the unit\n- **JOB** - Any pending job\n- **DESCRIPTION** - A human-readable description of the unit\n\n## Examples\n\n### List only failed units\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\n### List failed units in JSON format\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --output=json\n```\n\n### List failed units in JSON-pie format\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --output=json-pie\n```\n\n### List failed units in plain text\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --output=cat\n```\n\n## Additional Options\n\n### Show more details\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --no-pager\n```\n\n### List failed units for a specific service\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --type=service\n```\n\n### List failed units and their status\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --no-pager -l\n```\n\n## Common Use Cases\n\n1. **Troubleshooting**: After a system reboot, check for failed services\n2. **Monitoring**: Set up monitoring to alert on failed services\n3. **Debugging**: Investigate why a service failed\n\n## Related Commands\n\n- `systemctl list-units --state=failed` - Same as `--failed`\n- `systemctl status <unit>` - Check status of a specific unit\n- `journalctl -u <unit>` - View logs for a specific unit\n\nThe `--failed` option is equivalent to `--state=failed`, so both commands will produce the same output.",
